    <!-- 01 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="scope">
      <span class="legal-n">01</span>
      <div>
        <h2>What this covers</h2>
        <p>These terms cover this website and Prospektor Partner Studio — the tool where a sales team enters a target company, and the studio researches it, scores it as a partner, drafts material and lists the people worth approaching. Using either one means you accept them.</p>
        <p>If you're using the studio for work, you're agreeing on behalf of the company you work for, and you're telling us you're allowed to do that.</p>
        <p>What we do with the data is a separate page: <a href="/privacy/">privacy</a>. It's worth reading before you invite anyone.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 02 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="free">
      <span class="legal-n">02</span>
      <div>
        <h2>It's free right now</h2>
        <p>There is no plan, no payment step, no card and no invoice. We hold no billing data of any kind, because there is none to hold.</p>
        <p>We expect to charge for the studio at some point. If that happens we'll tell you before it applies to you, and you'll decide then whether to carry on. Nothing here signs you up to a future bill.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 03 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="accounts">
      <span class="legal-n">03</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Getting in</h2>
        <p>You sign in with Google. There's no password to choose and no other sign-in method. You get access if you're on a client's email allowlist, if you've been invited to create a workspace, or if you're holding a live share link.</p>
        <p>An account created by opening a share link gets you that one pitch and nothing else — it doesn't come with a workspace of its own.</p>
        <p>Your access is your Google account, so keep it secure. Anyone who has it, has your workspace.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 04 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="ai">
      <span class="legal-n">04</span>
      <div>
        <h2>The output is AI-generated, and it can be wrong</h2>
        <p>Everything the studio produces — the company research, the partner score, the list of decision-makers, the drafted material — is generated by an AI model. Treat it as a first draft from a fast, well-read colleague who is occasionally confidently wrong.</p>
        <p>There are guardrails. The model is instructed never to invent a figure, never to invent a person, and to cite a source for what it asserts. It re-reads its own draft against those rules and reports what it thinks it broke.</p>
        <p>Be clear about what that is and isn't:</p>
        <ul class="legal-list">
          <li>Instructions are not enforcement. Telling a model not to make things up is not the same as it being unable to.</li>
          <li>The compliance check <em>reports</em> problems. It doesn't silently fix them, and it can miss them.</li>
          <li>The decision-makers are real people. A wrong name, role or employer isn't a typo to the person it's about.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>So check it before it leaves your hands — every figure, every name, every claim about the target company. You're the last step, and it's your name on the email.</p>
        <p>We don't warrant that any output is accurate, complete, or fit for what you intend to do with it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 05 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="responsibility">
      <span class="legal-n">05</span>
      <div>
        <h2>What you're responsible for</h2>
        <p>You choose which companies to research, what to do with the result, and who to contact. Those are your decisions. You agree:</p>
        <ul class="legal-list">
          <li>to check the output before you use it outside your team;</li>
          <li>to follow the law that applies to you and to the people you approach — marketing, e-privacy and data protection rules included — and to handle a contact's objection properly when you get one;</li>
          <li><strong>not to use the studio for bulk unsolicited contact.</strong> It surfaces a handful of people worth a considered approach; it is not a list-building tool for mass outreach, and using it that way breaches these terms;</li>
          <li>not to use it to profile, monitor, harass or target individuals, or for anything other than a genuine business approach;</li>
          <li>not to resell, republish or redistribute the research or the contact data as a product of your own;</li>
          <li>not to enter data you don't have the right to use, including personal data you have no business reason to hold;</li>
          <li>not to attempt to reach another client's workspace, work around the access rules, or probe the service. If you find a way through, tell us — we'll thank you for it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 06 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="sharing">
      <span class="legal-n">06</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Sharing a pitch</h2>
        <p>A share link lets someone outside the workspace read one pitch. Two things follow from that.</p>
        <p>The first: a shared pitch carries your team's own prospecting on a named company — the research, the judgements, the drafted material. Sending it is your act, not ours. Think about who's on the other end before you do.</p>
        <p>The second: <strong>we record who opens it.</strong> Their email address, their name, when they first and last opened it, and how many times — and your team can see all of it. Anyone you send a link to should be told that, and it's on you to tell them. The detail is in the <a href="/privacy/#viewers">privacy policy</a>.</p>
        <p>You can revoke a link at any time. It stops working immediately for everyone, including people who already read it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 07 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="content">
      <span class="legal-n">07</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Your content</h2>
        <p>Your brief, your notes, your edits and the material the studio drafts for you are yours. We don't claim ownership of them, and we don't use one client's material to serve another.</p>
        <p>Two things happen inside your own workspace that are worth knowing:</p>
        <ul class="legal-list">
          <li>Drafts your team edits may be kept as brand-voice examples, so that later runs sound like you.</li>
          <li>Those examples outlive the pitch they came from — deleting the pitch doesn't delete them.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>We need your permission to store and process your content in order to run the service at all. That's the whole of the licence we take, and it ends when the data goes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="availability">
      <span class="legal-n">08</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Availability</h2>
        <p>This is early software, offered as it is. It can change, break, or be unavailable, and we don't promise uptime or give an SLA. We may add, change or remove features.</p>
        <p>There's no export button today, so if a piece of work matters to you, keep your own copy of it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 09 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="ending">
      <span class="legal-n">09</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Ending it</h2>
        <p>You can stop using the studio whenever you like. Ask us and we'll delete your account and its data — by hand, since there's no button for it yet. See <a href="/privacy/#retention">what deletion does and doesn't reach</a>.</p>
        <p>We can suspend or close an account that breaks <a href="#responsibility">section 05</a>, or where we're required to. If it's something fixable, we'll tell you what it is first, unless the circumstances don't allow that.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 10 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="liability">
      <span class="legal-n">10</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Liability, kept short</h2>
        <p>We provide the studio as it is, without warranties, as far as the law allows. We're not responsible for what you do with the output: if you send something inaccurate, approach someone you shouldn't have, or lose a deal, that sits with you.</p>
        <p>Nothing here excludes liability that can't legally be excluded — including for death or personal injury caused by negligence, or for fraud.</p>
        <p>If we do end up liable to you for something despite the above, the cap is what you've paid us for the service. Today that's nothing. We'd rather write that plainly than bury it in capital letters.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

